MT2	f450c965-4bb5-4198-b726-74c7969741b9	1	Wire high-score persistence across resets and render score/high score in the UI	Edit /workspace/repo/src/ui.js: introduce a module-level (or closure) variable, e.g. `let highScore = 0;`, that persists across calls to the 'New game' button handler for the lifetime of the page (in-memory only, no localStorage). Update the code path that currently calls `newGame(rng)` (both on initial load and inside the '#new-game' click handler) to call `newGame(rng, highScore)` instead, so the previous session's high score seeds the new game state. After every call that produces a new state — both the initial game creation and every tick inside the game loop's `step()` call — update the local `highScore` variable from `state.highScore` (so it stays in sync with the authoritative value computed by game.js), then update two DOM elements' text content: the current score element (id `score-value`) set to `state.score`, and the high score element (id `high-score-value`) set to `highScore`. Also edit /workspace/repo/index.html: inside the existing `<header>` (or immediately after the `<h1>Snake</h1>` and before/near the `#new-game` button — match existing structure), add a new element, e.g. `<p id="scoreboard">Score: <span id="score-value">0</span> &nbsp; High score: <span id="high-score-value">0</span></p>`, so both values are visible above the board at all times.	COMPLETED	1	[{"text": "On page load, the DOM elements with id 'score-value' and 'high-score-value' both show 0", "examples": []}, {"text": "After the snake eats one food item during play, the element with id 'score-value' updates to display 40, matching the score computed by game.js's step()", "examples": []}, {"text": "After reaching a score of 90 in a game and then clicking the 'New game' button, the score display resets to 0 while the high score display remains 90", "examples": []}, {"text": "After clicking 'New game' when the high score is 90, and then eating enough food in the new game to reach a score of 100, the high score display updates from 90 to 100", "examples": []}, {"text": "The scoreboard markup (score and high score elements) is positioned above the '#board' canvas element in index.html's DOM order", "examples": []}]	["MT1"]	[".gitignore", "index.html", "src/game.js", "src/ui.js", "test/game.test.js"]	Manually trace/verify via code inspection or a DOM-simulation check that renderScore()/element updates are called after initial load, after each tick's step(), and after the New game click handler, and that the new-game handler passes the retained highScore into newGame(), matching the exact 90-then-100 and 90-preserved-at-reset examples in the acceptance criteria.	2026-07-15 11:45:24.782736+00	2026-07-15 11:47:02.072482+00	["/workspace/repo/src/ui.js", "/workspace/repo/index.html"]	1) In ui.js, near the top where other mutable UI state (like the current `state` variable holding the game state) is declared, add `let highScore = 0;`. 2) Locate the initial call that creates the starting game state (e.g. `let state = newGame(rng);`) and change it to `let state = newGame(rng, highScore);`. 3) Locate the '#new-game' button's click event listener, which currently calls something like `state = newGame(rng);` on click — change this to `state = newGame(rng, highScore);` as well, so the reset seeds with the current in-memory high score. 4) Locate the tick/game-loop function that calls `state = step(state, rng);` on each interval tick — immediately after this line, add `highScore = state.highScore;` to keep the closure variable authoritative and in sync (game.js's step() is the source of truth for the max computation; ui.js just mirrors it). 5) Create a small render helper, e.g. `function renderScore() { document.getElementById('score-value').textContent = state.score; document.getElementById('high-score-value').textContent = highScore; }`, and call `renderScore()` at the end of the initial setup, at the end of the '#new-game' click handler, an